\chapter{Built-in Preprocessing and Optional Lua}

\texttt{P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r} transforms source text before rule/\allowbreak{}grammar parsing. Macro and conditional operations are built in. The user's include and script callbacks choose the source backend and host-language embedding policy.

\section{Start with object macros}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::Preprocessor pp;
auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules(pp.process(R"(
#define ZERO 0
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x ZERO) ?x)
)"));
assert(rules.size() == 1);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto pp = new Preprocessor;
auto rules = sexpr_rules(pp.process(`
#define ZERO 0
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x ZERO) ?x)
`));
assert(rules.length == 1);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The preprocessor also exposes \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} and the public \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s} table. An object macro expands identifier tokens. Strings, character literals and C-style comments preserve their contents. Expansion rescans replacement text; recursive expansion of the same macro is suppressed. For example, defining \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}F} as \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}F} leaves its use as \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}F}.

\texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} performs expansion alone. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} additionally handles directives and removes directive lines from output. The macro table persists across calls and included sources.

\section{Function macros and continued lines}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{text}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#define ID(x) x
#define ADD(x, y) (add x y)
ID(ADD(a, 0))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Function macro definitions require \texttt{(} immediately after the name. Invocations can contain whitespace before \texttt{(}. Arguments are separated at their outer comma level; balanced parentheses and quoted values preserve internal commas. Arguments expand before substitution, and the replacement is rescanned. Invalid parameter lists and mismatched argument counts raise exceptions.

\texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} provides programmatic registration. Backslash-newline pairs splice physical lines before directive processing, so a replacement can span several source lines.

The implemented function-macro subset has named parameters. Token-pasting, stringification and variadic macro syntax are outside the current API.

\section{Conditional source selection}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{text}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#define ENABLED 1
#if defined(ENABLED) && ENABLED == 1
(= (add ?x 0) ?x)
#elif defined(ALTERNATIVE)
(= (mul ?x 1) ?x)
#else
(= (sub ?x 0) ?x)
#endif
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Supported directives are \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} and \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}. Nested blocks maintain parent activity and previous branch selection. Inactive branches do not define macros, resolve includes or execute scripts. Unmatched, duplicate or unterminated conditional blocks raise exceptions.

Conditions accept decimal/\allowbreak{}hexadecimal integer constants, identifiers, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} with or without parentheses, logical \texttt{!}, parentheses, comparisons \texttt{=\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}=}, and \texttt{\&\allowbreak{}\&}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{|\allowbreak{}|}. Undefined identifiers evaluate to zero. Object-macro replacements used in conditions are evaluated recursively. Arithmetic, bitwise expressions and function-macro evaluation in conditions are outside the implemented expression subset.

\texttt{\#\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}E} removes a macro. Active \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e} raises an exception; inactive \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r} directives are skipped. Unknown active directives are rejected.

\section{Resolve includes through a backend}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
pp.include = [](std::string_view name) -> std::string {
    if (name == "common.rules") return "#define ZERO 0\n";
    throw std::runtime_error("unknown include");
};
auto output = pp.process("#include \"common.rules\"\n(= (add ?x ZERO) ?x)\n");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
pp.include = (string name) {
    if (name == "common.rules") return "#define ZERO 0\n";
    throw new Exception("unknown include");
};
auto output = pp.process("#include \"common.rules\"\n(= (add ?x ZERO) ?x)\n");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Paths may be quoted or angle-bracketed; the resolver receives the path without delimiters. Include text is processed recursively and shares the macro table. The toolkit does not select filesystem search directories or a concrete I/\allowbreak{}O backend. A resolver may compose the abstract I/\allowbreak{}O facilities in Chapter 33.

\texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h} defaults to 100 and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h} to 32. Exceeding either raises an exception. Exceptions from user callbacks propagate.

\section{Supply a script backend}

\texttt{s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} returns generated text for \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}. \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} is shorthand for language \texttt{l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a}. The preprocessor macro-expands the returned text. It does not re-run generated text as a set of directives. Supply a trailing newline deliberately when returning several generated lines.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
pp.script = (string language, string code) {
    assert(language == "lua" && code == "emit_rule");
    return "(= (add ?x ZERO) ?x)";
};
assert(sexpr_rules(pp.process("#lua emit_rule\n")).length == 1);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

This callback contract is usable for any scripting engine. The native Lua bridge implements it for a user-owned Lua state.

\section{Enable native Lua in C++}

Define \texttt{M\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}A} before including the header and supply Lua's include and link flags:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#define MUDSL_ENABLE_LUA
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cassert>

int main() {
    auto state = luaL_newstate();
    assert(state);
    dsl::Preprocessor pp;
    pp.script = dsl::lua_script_hook(state);
    assert(pp.process("#lua return '(= a b)'\n") == "(= a b)\n");
    assert(lua_gettop(state) == 0);
    lua_close(state);
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
# From the MuDSL directory, with the example saved as lua-app.cpp:
g++ -std=c++20 -I. lua-app.cpp $(pkg-config --cflags --libs lua5.4) -o lua-app
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Use the pkg-config name supplied by the installed Lua package. The bridge uses the supplied state; opening libraries and configuring it are user operations.

\section{Enable native Lua in D}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import MuDSL;

void main()
{
    auto state = luaL_newstate();
    assert(state !is null);
    scope(exit) lua_close(state);
    auto pp = new Preprocessor;
    pp.script = lua_script_hook(state);
    assert(pp.process("#lua return '(= a b)'\n") == "(= a b)\n");
    assert(lua_gettop(state) == 0);
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
ldc2 -d-version=MuDSLLua lua-app.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -L-llua5.4 -of=lua-app
dmd -version=MuDSLLua lua-app.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -L-llua5.4 -of=lua-app
gdc -fversion=MuDSLLua lua-app.d MuDSL.d -llua5.4 -o lua-app
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The D declarations target the Lua 5.3/\allowbreak{}5.4 ABI. A directive loads and executes its code with one return value, obtains generated text, and restores the original stack height after success or failure. Keep the state alive while its hook is used. Configure standard libraries with \texttt{l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}s} when the script needs them. The bridge is optional; default builds use the standard library only.

\section{Verify preprocessing}

The ordinary facility tests cover macros, inactive branches, comments, continuations, include resolution and callback-based scripts. To enable the native C++ bridge test, configure the standalone test project with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}N}. For D native-bridge tests:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
ldc2 -unittest -main -d-version=MuDSLLua MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -L-llua5.4 -of=mudsl-lua-test
./mudsl-lua-test
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup
